      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "CO2 laser emitted radiant heat pulses of 20 ms duration were used to activate predominantly slowly conducting nociceptive cutaneous afferents in man. Stimuli of two-fold individual pain threshold caused stinging and burning pain and elicited cerebral potentials with latencies consistent with A delta-fibre activity. After preferential block of the myelinated nerve fibres by pressure only the burning pain remained with significantly increased reaction time (about 1433 ms). The A delta-fibre-induced evoked potential components disappeared, and a marked ultralate positive component became visible with mean peak latency of 1260 ms, consistent with C-fibre activity."},
